import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Button
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/Trace
import ../../Ui/Who
import ../../Ui/Arch
import ../../Ui/ArchBox

meta = {
	title: "The platform",
	description: "What a rocx app runs on: a server platform, Roc compiled to native code in a Rust host with Postgres, jobs, email and actors; and a browser platform, the islands in WebAssembly.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "two", label: "Two platforms, one app" },
		{ id: "server", label: "What the server gives" },
		{ id: "app", label: "An app" },
		{ id: "request", label: "A request, all the way" },
		{ id: "build", label: "Building and running" },
		{ id: "why", label: "Why this shape" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform"
				title="Two platforms, one app: native on the server, wasm in the browser"
				actions={[
					<Button href={Paths.platform_server} primary>The server</Button>,
					<Button href={Paths.platform_browser}>The browser</Button>,
				]}
			>
				A Roc program runs on a platform: the host that gives it the effects it may have. A rocx app is
				two programs. The server's is Roc compiled to native code, linked with a Rust host into one
				executable, which answers HTTP and does what a single server does: Postgres, jobs, cron, email,
				state that outlives a request. The browser's is the app's islands, compiled to WebAssembly, with a
				small Zig host and a page of JavaScript. The two talk over HTTP.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="two">Two platforms, one app</Heading>
			<Arch
				left={[
					<ArchBox kind="roc" name={[<code>server/</code>, <>: native</>]}>
						<p>
							Roc compiled to machine code, in a Rust host (tokio, hyper). Pages and endpoints, the islands
							rendered into pages; Postgres, jobs and cron, email, live rooms, actors. One executable, one
							server.
						</p>
					</ArchBox>,
				]}
				wire="HTTP"
				right={[
					<ArchBox kind="wasm" name={[<code>web/</code>, <>: wasm</>]}>
						<p>
							The islands only, compiled to WebAssembly with a Zig host, and <code>web/runtime.js</code>, which
							hydrates them, runs their holes again when signals change, and sends their requests.
						</p>
					</ArchBox>,
				]}
			/>
			<p>
				The same components compile on both: the modules they are written with (<code>Html</code>,
				<code>Solid</code>, <code>Show</code>, <code>For</code>, <code>Loading</code>, <code>Errored</code>,
				<code>Context</code>, <code>Island</code>, <code>Server</code>, <code>Call</code>) are the browser
				platform's, and the build copies them into the server platform, whose host renders them as the
				browser's runtime would, byte for byte.
			</p>

			<Heading id="server">What the server gives</Heading>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Requests and streams" href={Paths.platform_server}>
					<p>
						Every request a call of its own, which pauses while it waits; streams of events as loops that
						sleep; a crash that ends one call, not the server, and frees what it held.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="HTTP, both ways" href={Paths.platform_http}>
					<p>
						Requests to other servers, with limits; bodies as the bytes they were; answers fetched from elsewhere
						and passed on as they come; cookies with every attribute.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Settings and crypto" href={Paths.platform_secrets}>
					<p>
						The environment, a dev server's own paths, random bytes and AES-GCM, and the crypto package an
						app signs with: SHA-256, HMAC, HKDF.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Postgres" href={Paths.platform_postgres}>
					<p>
						Queries whose rows decode into the record you annotate, transactions, migrations built into the
						app; or roc-pg, whose queries are checked at build.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Jobs, cron and email" href={Paths.platform_jobs}>
					<p>
						Work queued in Postgres and run by workers, again if it fails; schedules; email over SMTP, or an
						outbox while developing.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Live and actors" href={Paths.platform_live}>
					<p>
						Topics and rooms streams share (who is here, what was said), and actors: state one message at a
						time, kept in Postgres.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>
			<p>
				It is opinionated on purpose: one server, Roc and Postgres. The host, in Rust, gives Roc general
				things: HTTP, Postgres connections, sockets with TLS, background loops that sleep until woken, calls
				isolated from their caller, actors' mailboxes and timers. Jobs, cron, migrations, email and actors'
				state are the platform's Roc, written on those
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#layers"}>what is Rust, what is Roc</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="app">An app</Heading>
			<p>
				On the server platform an app is a record: how it answers a request, its migrations, the kinds of
				job its workers run, its schedules, and its islands. The platform's tests' app has all of them:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_program.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_program.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>This site has pages, endpoints and islands, and none of the rest:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_site_program.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_site_program.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>respond!</code> takes a request's method, target, headers, body and what the server was
				started with (<code>--context-file</code>), and answers with a <code>Server.Response</code>:
				<code>Server.from_http</code> makes the request, and the routes rocx writes answer it
				(<a href={Paths.routing}>routing</a>). An answer that is a stream carries it: a function the host
				runs as a call of its own, with its <code>send!</code> and <code>sleep!</code>. <code>actors</code>
				lists the app's kinds of actor, which <code>rocx build</code> finds, so the host can run one when
				nobody waits on it (a tell, a timer). The browser's app is the islands alone:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.client_app.file} source={Sources.client_app.rocx} />

			<Heading id="request">A request, all the way</Heading>
			<Trace>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> hyper hands the host a request; the host reads its body
					(16 MiB at most) and gives it to Roc, a call of its own on one of Roc's threads (it pauses
					whenever an effect waits), inside a guard that catches a crash.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> The app's <code>respond!</code> runs its middleware and
					routes, and renders the page: plain HTML, with a <code>{"<rocx-island data-island=\"todos\">"}</code>
					where an island goes. Its effects (a query, a job queued) block that thread, and no other.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> The host sees the page has islands, and runs the app's
					islands' root: each island the page has is rendered into its place, the endpoints it reads
					answered there and then, in-process, and their answers put at the end of the page.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> The page shows at once, islands and all. Its script
					finds the islands, fetches the wasm, and hydrates them: the same components run again, bound to the
					server's nodes, reading the answers from the page instead of asking.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> A click writes a signal; what reads it runs again and
					patches its own node. A read whose answer isn't in the page (its URL built from a signal, or
					read again with <code>refresh!</code>) is a GET of the endpoint's own URL, which the server's app
					answers through the same route.
				</li>
			</Trace>

			<Heading id="build">Building and running</Heading>
			<Code
				title="In this repository"
				source={"just db        # a Postgres in Docker; prints its DATABASE_URL\njust dev       # this site on it: http://localhost:8080, rebuilt on save\nnode server/build.mjs app.roc out/app [--opt=speed] [--target=arm64musl]"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>server/build.mjs</code> builds the host with cargo into
				<code>server/platform/targets/</code>, then the app with <code>roc build</code>, which links the two
				into one executable. On macOS it links against the system; <code>--target=arm64musl</code> (or
				<code>x64musl</code>) makes a static Linux executable, cross-compiled, to copy into a container. The
				executable takes its port, its database and the rest as flags or environment
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#running"}>running</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="why">Why this shape</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Native on the server.</strong> Roc compiles to machine code, so the server runs it as such:
					threads for every request, effects that block a thread and nothing else, and Rust where Roc can't
					reach (sockets, TLS, threads). No wasm runtime between them.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Wasm in the browser.</strong> The browser runs WebAssembly, and only the islands go there:
					no page's markup, and nothing an endpoint's handler does.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One component model.</strong> Islands are Solid-style components on both, so the server
					renders what the browser will hydrate, with the answers it will need.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One server.</strong> Live rooms, topics and actors' mailboxes are in its memory; jobs and
					actors' state are in Postgres already. Several servers are on the roadmap, not here yet.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="note" title="A working prototype">
				<p>
					Both platforms are tested, together and apart: the server's against Postgres, crashes and all; the
					browser's in Chromium; this site on both. Each page here says what isn't there yet.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
